@@ -0,0 +1,85 @@
+# Provision one workspace per brand (restaurant groups)
+
+`scripts/provision-workspaces.ts` sets up a group of brands from a private JSON file. Each brand gets its own workspace (`Client`), with one `BusinessBrand` and its `BusinessLocation`s. The group admin owns every workspace, so one login manages all the brands. Each brand's own owner gets an owner invitation.
+
+Membership is per workspace. Brand-level access does not exist, so a separate workspace per brand is the only way to keep each brand owner out of the other brands.
+
+## Rules
+
+- **Dry-run by default.** It prints what it would create or skip, and why anything is blocked. It writes nothing.
+- **The data file stays outside this repository.** The repository is public. The script refuses a data file inside it. The only exception is the fake example (`scripts/examples/provision-workspaces.example.json`), and only in dry-run. `--apply` also refuses `example.com` / `.test` addresses, except against a local database.
+- **Writes go through the app's services:** `createClient`, `createBrand` and `createLocation`. They keep their own checks and audit rows: `workspace_created`, `business_brand_created` and `business_location_created`.
+- **Plan gates are never bypassed.** The script never writes `ClientSubscription`, `planName` or `developmentBypass`.
+- **Nobody gets a password.** Invited people set their own password through the invitation link.
+- **Output is safe to share.** Emails are masked unless you pass `--show-emails`. Tokens, token hashes, links, keys and the connection string are never printed.
+
+## Run it
+
+```bash
+# 1. Validate the file only (no database needed)
+npm run provision:workspaces -- --data ~/private/group.json
+
+# 2. Dry-run against a database (env from a private file, never committed)
+npx tsx --env-file=$HOME/private/staging.env --require ./scripts/register-server-only.cjs \
+  scripts/provision-workspaces.ts --data ~/private/group.json --actor-email <your platform admin email>
+
+# 3. Write. --confirm-db must equal the DATABASE_URL hostname.
+npx tsx --env-file=... --require ./scripts/register-server-only.cjs scripts/provision-workspaces.ts \
+  --data ~/private/group.json --actor-email <you> --apply --confirm-db <db hostname>
+```
+
+The other options are `--only key1,key2`, `--initial-owner admin|actor`, `--send-invites` (also accepted as `--send-emails`), `--invite-delay-seconds` (default 65), `--max-invites` (default 10), `--reinvite-expired`, `--json` and `--show-emails`. Run with `--help` for details.
+
+Exit codes:
+
+- `0`: no errors. BLOCKED, DEFERRED and UNSUPPORTED items do not count as errors.
+- `1`: a validation, precondition, conflict or write error.
+- `2`: bad usage.
+
+## Data file (version 1)
+
+Start from the example. The main parts are:
+
+- `group`: `companyName`, `adminEmail` and an optional `assignedPlatformAdminEmail`.
+- `defaults`: timezone, country, region, city, statuses and category.
+- `addresses`: named addresses that locations can share.
+- `hours`: named weekly schedules. Each day takes `[["16:00","03:00"]]`, and a closing time earlier than the opening time means past midnight.
+- `brands[]`: each brand has `key`, `brand.name` and `locations`. It can also have `workspaceName` (default: the brand name), `ownerEmail`, `workspaceEmail` (default: null) and `hours`.
+
+Unknown fields are rejected. A location without a name is called `<brand> - <address line 1>`. The brand takes the address of its primary location.
+
+## What each status means
+
+| Status | Meaning |
+| --- | --- |
+| CREATE / SKIP | Will be created / already exists. Nothing is created twice. Workspaces are matched by name, case-insensitive, and must be owned by the admin or the actor. Brands are matched by name inside the workspace. Locations are matched by address or by name. Invitations are matched by workspace and email. |
+| BLOCKED | Waits for something. The line says what unblocks it: a sign-in, a plan, or `--reinvite-expired`. |
+| CONFLICT | Needs a human. Examples: a workspace with the same name that belongs to someone else, a workspace email already in use, or a location name at another address. |
+| DEFERRED | Only one live invitation per email at a time, or the `--max-invites` cap was reached. Run the script again later. |
+| UNSUPPORTED | Opening hours. The schema has no field for them, so they are validated but not stored. Storing them needs its own schema pull request. |
+| PLANNED / SEND | Owner invitation is ready / will be sent with `--apply --send-invites`. |
+
+## Plans: what a fresh workspace can do
+
+A new workspace has no Social subscription, so:
+
+- **Brands:** the brand allowance is 0. A first `--apply` creates the workspace and its owner, then reports the brand and its locations as BLOCKED. **Social Essential** or higher in that workspace's Plans & billing page unblocks the brand.
+- **Team invitations:** these need **Advanced 15/25/50** or a Custom contract (`assertClientCanInviteTeam`). Essential and Starter do not include team management.
+
+At the end, the script prints a "Blocked by the Social plan" table with the cheapest plan that unblocks each workspace. Run it again after buying the plans: it only adds what is missing.
+
+## Owners and invitations
+
+- **Default (`--initial-owner admin`).** The group admin becomes the owner through `createClient`, with no email, so the admin must already have an active account (signed in once). Until then, new workspaces are BLOCKED.
+- **`--initial-owner actor`.** The Platform admin running the script becomes the initial owner, and the group admin is queued for an owner invitation instead.
+- **Delivery.** Invitations follow the same steps as `POST /api/team/invitations`, through the same Supabase calls: a 7-day expiry, a Supabase invite or, for an existing account, a magic link, then a revoke plus audit on failure. The difference is that the inviter is the Platform Owner/Admin running the script, where the app requires a workspace owner.
+- **One email at a time.** Each new invite or magic link to the same email replaces the previous link. The script therefore keeps one live invitation per email, pauses between sends, and never replaces a pending invitation.
+- **Requirements for `--send-invites`:**
+  - `NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL` set to a public https origin;
+  - the Supabase admin settings;
+  - the redirect allow-list and the email template that the app's own invitations already use.
+
+## Checks
+
+- `npm run qa:provision-workspaces`: pure tests (data file, address matching, masking, statuses, plan gates).
+- `npm run qa:provision-workspaces-db`: runs against a loopback database only (CI's ephemeral Supabase, or a local Postgres after `prisma db push`). It covers dry-run, apply, idempotent re-runs, plan unlocks, the mirrored invitation with a fake Supabase client, and the command-line guards.
